The completion of the negative displacement patch.

The first two patches declare their options with a value, so the compiler
refuses them: an option that takes no value is declared without the '='.
This patch fixes the two declarations and completes the work on the
displacement: the sign is not lost when the tree layer decomposes an
address into base and offset - it is the extension of the address to the
target's mode that hides the "base + constant" from the backend - so the
rule belongs where the extension happens, in explow.c.

The files it touches - gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt and gcc/explow.c - are
upstream GNU Compiler Collection, and each carries the Free Software
Foundation's notice:

    This file is part of GCC.  GCC is free software; you can redistribute
    it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public
    License as published by the Free Software Foundation; either version
    2, or (at your option) any later version.

A modification of a GPL file is under the GPL, so this patch is published
under the same terms, with the notices kept.  It touches no file that Sony
added to GCC, and quotes no SDK header, library or sample.
The completion of the second patch: the address of a memory reference.

Two changes, both measured.

1. The two options of the second patch are declared as plain switches.
   "mps3-toc-direct=" and "mps3-neg-disp=" are options that take a
   value, and GCC then refuses both "-mps3-toc-direct" and
   "-mps3-toc-direct=1" ("unrecognized command line option").

2. The address of a memory reference keeps the pointer mode when it is a
   "base + constant" whose constant is a signed 16 bit quantity.  The
   extension to Pmode is what hides the "base + constant" from the
   backend: the address the instruction generator sees is a register, and
   the displacement is computed in 64 bits and truncated ("addi" and
   "rldicl") instead of being folded into the instruction
   ("lwz rX, -0x7fec(r30)").

   The change is in gcc/explow.c, memory_address(), and it is behind
   -mps3-neg-disp: the behaviour of the SDK is unchanged by default.

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt
@@ -318,10 +318,10 @@
 ; And it folds a negative displacement into the load, where the D-form's
 ; displacement is tested as unsigned and the address is computed in 64 bits
 ; then truncated.
-mps3-toc-direct=
+mps3-toc-direct
 Target Var(ps3_toc_direct) Init(0)
 Read the entry of the TOC in one instruction, from r2.
 
-mps3-neg-disp=
+mps3-neg-disp
 Target Var(ps3_neg_disp) Init(0)
 Fold a negative displacement into the load.
--- a/gcc/explow.c
+++ b/gcc/explow.c
@@ -405,7 +405,26 @@
 {
   rtx oldx = x;
 
-  x = convert_memory_address (Pmode, x);
+  /* The address of a memory reference is built in the pointer mode, which
+     is 32 bits wide on this target, and it is then extended to Pmode
+     (64 bits) so that it can be used as an address.  The extension is
+     what hides the "base + constant" from the backend: the address the
+     instruction generator sees is a register, and the displacement is
+     computed in 64 bits and truncated ("addi" and "rldicl") instead of
+     being folded into the instruction ("lwz rX, -0x7fec(r30)").
+
+     The extension is redundant when the address is a "base + constant"
+     whose constant is a signed 16 bit quantity: the D-form computes
+     (base + constant) modulo 2^32 anyway.  -mps3-neg-disp selects the
+     retail.s behaviour.  */
+  if (!(ps3_neg_disp
+	  && GET_MODE (x) == ptr_mode
+	  && GET_CODE (x) == PLUS
+	  && GET_CODE (XEXP (x, 0)) == REG
+	  && GET_CODE (XEXP (x, 1)) == CONST_INT
+	  && INTVAL (XEXP (x, 1)) >= -32768
+	  && INTVAL (XEXP (x, 1)) <= 32767))
+    x = convert_memory_address (Pmode, x);
 
   /* By passing constant addresses through registers
      we get a chance to cse them.  */
